1. The Problem Interceptors Solve
So far, every API call has been written independently in each component or service.
If 10 APIs need the Authorization header, we copy the same header code 10 times.
If 10 APIs need 401 handling, we copy the same error code 10 times.
If we want to log every request, we repeat console.log everywhere.
This is repeated code. It is hard to maintain, and if one place is forgotten, a bug appears.
We need one single place that can act on all requests and responses.
That place is an Interceptor.
2. What is an HTTP Interceptor
An interceptor is a function that sits between the application and the server.
Every HTTP request made using HttpClient passes through it before going to the server.
Every response coming back from the server also passes through it before reaching the component.
It can read, modify, block, or observe requests and responses.
It is an Angular HttpClient concept. It is not a feature of HTTP or of the server.
The server does not know an interceptor exists. It only sees the final request.
3. Real-Life Analogy
Security gate of an office building.
Everyone entering or leaving passes through one gate.
The guard checks the ID, records the entry, and may stop someone.
Individual departments (components) do not keep their own guards.
Similarly, one interceptor handles all HTTP traffic.
4. Where It Sits in the Flow
Component
    ↓
HttpClient.get()
    ↓
Interceptor  (request goes through here)
    ↓
Server
    ↓
Interceptor  (response comes back through here)
    ↓
Component → subscribe()
The interceptor sees the request on the way out.
The interceptor sees the response on the way back.
The component does not know the interceptor exists, so component code stays clean.
5. Two Sides of an Interceptor
Side	When it runs	Typical use
Request side	Before the request goes to the server	Add token, add headers, log the request, show loading spinner
Response side	After the response comes back	Handle 401, show error messages, log the response, hide loading spinner
One interceptor can do both sides.
6. Common Real-World Uses
Attach the Authorization token to every request.
Handle 401 in one place (clear token, redirect to login).
Handle global errors (show a toast or message).
Show and hide a loading spinner for all API calls.
Log requests and responses for debugging.
Add common headers (for example Accept, language).
Add a base URL so that components do not repeat it.
Retry failed requests globally.
